Few things affect a coin's grade, or its appeal to collectors, as much as cleaning. A coin that has been scrubbed, polished or dipped can never be returned to its original surface, and an experienced eye will usually notice. If you are about to buy a coin, or thinking of sending one in for grading, it pays to know what to look for.

## Why cleaning matters

When a coin is struck, the metal flows outward from the center of the die and leaves microscopic flow lines on its surface. Those lines catch the light and produce luster: the soft, rotating glow that collectors call the "cartwheel". Over decades, the surface also develops toning, a natural layer of color that forms as the metal reacts slowly with its surroundings.

Cleaning disturbs both. Abrasives, brushes and even a cloth wipe cut fine scratches across the flow lines, and chemical dips strip away the outermost layer of metal along with the toning. The result may look bright at arm's length, but it is no longer the surface the coin was made with, and that is why a cleaned coin is usually described with a details grade rather than a straight numeric grade. You can read more about details grades in our [grading standards](/grading-standards).

## How to look at a coin

Most signs of cleaning are invisible under a broad, diffuse light such as an overhead ceiling lamp. To see them:

- Use a single, point light source, such as a desk lamp with a plain bulb, held about a foot above the coin.
- Hold the coin by its edges and tilt it slowly under the light, then rotate it, so the light sweeps across every part of the surface.
- Use a loupe of around five to seven power for an overall look, and a stronger one to confirm anything you suspect.
- Look at both sides, and at the edge and rims as well as the fields and devices.

## The signs to look for

### Hairlines

Fine, parallel scratches running in the same direction across the fields are the classic sign of a coin that has been wiped or brushed. They are often easiest to see in the open, flat areas of the design, and they appear and disappear as you tilt the coin. A few random marks are normal on any coin; a pattern of parallel lines is not.

### Broken or missing luster

On an original coin with luster, the cartwheel rolls smoothly around the coin as you tilt it. On a cleaned coin it may be broken up, patchy or missing altogether, and the surface can look flat, dull or "washed out" even though it is bright.

### Unnatural color

A coin that is too bright for its age deserves a second look. So does color that seems wrong for the metal: a copper coin that is an unnatural salmon or pinkish orange, or silver that looks chalky white or harshly brilliant. Original toning tends to deepen gradually from the protected areas outward; color that stops abruptly, or sits only on the high points, can be a sign that something has been done to the surface.

### Residue in the protected areas

Look closely around the letters, the date and the edges of the design. Cleaning compounds and polish often leave a residue in these recesses, and the color there may not match the fields. On a coin that has been partly cleaned, the protected areas can keep their old toning while the open fields are bright.

### Whizzing and other alteration

Whizzing is a more serious form of alteration in which a rotating wire brush is used to move the metal itself, creating a false imitation of luster. Under magnification the surface looks granular or swirled, the edges of letters and devices are softened or rounded, and metal may appear to have been pushed up against the raised design. Coins that have been whizzed, polished or otherwise altered are among the hardest to sell, which is exactly why the alteration is sometimes disguised.

## Cleaning is not the same as conservation

Not every change to a surface is damage. Removing loose dirt, or residue from old plastic holders, with inert solvents that do not attack the metal is generally considered conservation rather than cleaning, because the original surface is left as it was. The line between the two is not always obvious, and a careful judgement of the coin in hand is what decides it. That is one of the reasons we note problems such as cleaning plainly, rather than hiding them, at the [grading stage of our process](/process).

## Never clean a coin yourself

If you take one thing away from this article, let it be this: do not try to clean a coin before you send it in. Even gentle methods, such as a soft toothbrush, a cloth or a household cleaner, leave marks that cannot be undone, and a coin that might have received a numeric grade can end up with a details grade instead. A coin with honest, original toning is almost always better left exactly as it is.

If you are unsure whether a coin has been cleaned, or whether it is worth submitting, [ask us before you send it](/submit). We would much rather have that conversation first.
